  "program": "CBTRN03C",
  "caseId": "case09",
  "description": "THE FIRST PAGING BOUNDARY - the one place in app/cbl/CBTRN03C.cbl where FUNCTION MOD(WS-LINE-COUNTER, WS-PAGE-SIZE) at :282 is true. WS-LINE-COUNTER is PIC 9(09) COMP-3 VALUE 0 at :129-130 and WS-PAGE-SIZE is PIC 9(03) COMP-3 VALUE 20 at :131-132; both are BEHAVIOUR AND NOT CONFIGURATION, so 20 is what the report does rather than a tunable, and they are also the program's only packed-decimal items - no copybook in the repository declares COMP-3, so no packed unpacking enters the record codec and these two are an in-memory counter and bound. Seventeen in-range transactions all carry TRAN-CARD-NUM 0500024453765740, row 1 of app/data/ASCII/cardxref.txt, whose XREF-ACCT-ID is 00000000050, so the guard at :181 is true exactly once, no account break ever intrudes, and the counter walk is clean. THE WALK, derived paragraph by paragraph: record 1 finds WS-FIRST-TIME 'Y' at :275, so 1120-WRITE-HEADERS (:324-341) writes four records and takes the counter 0 -> 4; the MOD test then evaluates MOD(4,20) = 4 and breaks no page; 1120-WRITE-DETAIL (:361-374) takes it to 5. Records 2 through 16 enter 1100-WRITE-TRANSACTION-REPORT with the counter at 5, 6, ... 19, every MOD non-zero, one detail line each, ending at 20. RECORD 17 ENTERS WITH THE COUNTER AT 20 AND MOD(20,20) = 0, so the trigger fires for the first and only time in the run: 1110-WRITE-PAGE-TOTALS (:293-304) writes REPORT-PAGE-TOTALS and its trailing TRANSACTION-HEADER-2 rule and takes 20 -> 22, then 1120-WRITE-HEADERS writes a fresh four-record header block and takes 22 -> 26, then :287-288 add the amount to a page total the trigger has just zeroed, then the detail takes it to 27. The end-of-file arm at :197-204 adds two more, 27 -> 29, and 1110-WRITE-GRAND-TOTALS (:318-322) writes the final record WITHOUT incrementing, which is the only paragraph in the program that does not. So THIRTY records leave the writer while WS-LINE-COUNTER ends at TWENTY-NINE, and the difference of exactly one is that missing increment. THE ORDERING IS LOAD-BEARING IN TWO PLACES. First, the counter is 0 when record 1 arrives and MOD(0,20) is itself zero, so a translation that hoisted the MOD test above the WS-FIRST-TIME block would break a page before a single line existed and open the report with a page total of zero; the first-time block runs FIRST, pushes the counter to 4, and the trigger therefore cannot fire on record 1. Second, inside the trigger the page total is written BEFORE the new headers (:283 then :284), so REPORT-PAGE-TOTALS closes the old page and the header block opens the new one - swap those two PERFORMs and the thirty records still number thirty but read as a report with its headers in the wrong place, which is why the expected order here is headers, sixteen details, page total and rule, headers, detail 17, page total and rule, grand total. THE ARITHMETIC, scale 2 throughout because WS-PAGE-TOTAL, WS-ACCOUNT-TOTAL and WS-GRAND-TOTAL are all PIC S9(09)V99 at :134-136 and every store truncates toward zero - ROUNDED appears zero times in CBTRN03C and zero times in any of the 28 programs. Records 1 to 16 carry 100.00, 200.00 ... 1600.00, an arithmetic series summing to 100.00 x 136 = 13,600.00, which is the FIRST PAGE TOTAL written at row 20 as '+     13,600.00'; :297 rolls it into the grand total and :298 zeroes the page total. That series also crosses the mask's thousands comma inside a single page, so record 9 renders '         900.00' and record 10 '       1,000.00'. Record 17 carries 750.25, added to the now-zero page total, so the SECOND PAGE TOTAL ought to be 750.25 and reads 1,500.50 instead. PRESERVED DEFECT 2 (practice B5): a COBOL READ ... INTO leaves its receiving area untouched AT END, so when the read at :249 reaches end of file TRAN-RECORD still holds record 17, and the ELSE arm at :197-204 adds its TRAN-AMT a SECOND time at :200-201 to a page total that already contains it. The second page total is therefore exactly twice amount 17 - 750.25 + 750.25 = 1,500.50 - and the GRAND TOTAL is (amounts 1 to 16) + 2 x amount 17 = 13,600.00 + 1,500.50 = 15,100.50, written as '+     15,100.50'. That is the clearest statement of the defect this folder can make: with one duplicated amount, every figure in the report except the sixteen first-page details is overstated. It is inherited legacy behaviour, deliberately reproduced and NEVER repaired, because correcting it would change every report this program has ever produced - a behaviour change and a parity violation. WS-ACCOUNT-TOTAL reaches the same 15,100.50 and IS NEVER WRITTEN: 1120-WRITE-ACCOUNT-TOTALS (:306-316) has exactly one caller, the account break at :183, which requires a card change, and the card never changes; the end-of-file arm performs only 1110-WRITE-PAGE-TOTALS and 1110-WRITE-GRAND-TOTALS. SO NO 'Account Total' LINE APPEARS ANYWHERE IN THESE THIRTY RECORDS - supplying one, which is exactly the line a helpful implementation adds, is a difference this case reports. THE TWO DIAGNOSTIC LINES AT :198-199 ARE THE SUBTLE ASSERTION. Both fire BEFORE the duplicate add, so DISPLAY 'TRAN-AMT ' TRAN-AMT reports 0000007502E - the raw zoned image of 750.25 with the sign overpunched into the trailing byte - and DISPLAY 'WS-PAGE-TOTAL' WS-PAGE-TOTAL reports the SAME 0000007502E, because the trigger zeroed the page total sixteen records into the run and it now holds amount 17 and nothing else. The two images are identical by construction rather than by coincidence: a page total that still carried the first page's 13,600.00 would read 0000136000{ instead, and a page total the trigger had failed to zero would read 0000143502E. The literals differ - 'TRAN-AMT ' carries a trailing space and 'WS-PAGE-TOTAL' does not. GEOMETRY. Every one of the thirty records is the 133 bytes app/jcl/TRANREPT.jcl:78 and app/proc/TRANREPT.prc:76 both declare as DCB=(LRECL=133,RECFM=FB,BLKSIZE=0): the two 115-byte REPORT-NAME-HEADERs are right-space-padded by 18, the two 114-byte TRANSACTION-HEADER-1 lines and the seventeen 114-byte detail lines by 19, and the two 112-byte REPORT-PAGE-TOTALS and the 112-byte REPORT-GRAND-TOTALS by 21, while the four TRANSACTION-HEADER-2 rules (PIC X(133) VALUE ALL '-') and the two WS-BLANK-LINEs (PIC X(133) VALUE SPACES at :133) arrive already at full width. Each total line keeps its FILLER PIC X(86) VALUE ALL '.' leader intact, all 86 dots of it. All seventeen detail lines carry 00000000050 at bytes 18-28 from the single cross-reference read the :181 guard allows, and the '-' bytes at 32 and 53 survive INITIALIZE at :362 because COBOL leaves FILLER untouched. Record 13's category description 'Online purchase authorization' is exactly 29 characters and so fills TRAN-REPORT-CAT-DESC to its last byte without truncating - TranReportWriterTest's dedicated nested class owns the two truncating moves, no declarative case in this folder does. The second header block still shows the same date range, because REPT-START-DATE and REPT-END-DATE were moved once at :277-278 and are never cleared. Both EVALUATE statements are exercised on '00' and on '10' and neither reaches WHEN OTHER; nothing abends, so RETURN-CODE stays 0. CARDXREF is seeded from the 36-byte fixture UNPADDED and the declared CARDXREF_FILLER_PAD_36_TO_50 normalisation widens it to the 50 bytes app/cpy/CVACT03Y.cpy declares, so the fixture's absent trailing FILLER X(14) is padded at seeding time rather than hidden (gate G16, risk R-F). The reporting range is read from the DATEPARM dataset and this case declares NO job parameter, because app/jcl/TRANREPT.jcl gives STEP10R no PARM at all. PROVENANCE (practice B12): every value here is STATICALLY DERIVED by structured reading of the COBOL, cross-checked against the byte layouts of CVTRA05Y, CVTRA03Y, CVTRA04Y, CVACT03Y and CVTRA07Y, the DD and DCB contracts of TRANREPT.jcl and TRANREPT.prc, and the real US-ASCII fixtures in app/data/ASCII - it is NEVER captured from an execution of the legacy COBOL, which AAP section 0.7.6 documents as empirically impossible here across eight verified blockers, and that substitution of provenance alone is escalated as risk R-A rather than silently absorbed. review_rules returns exactly one line, \"No user rules provided.\", so no user rule governs this fixture; the binding standard is the enterprise-practice set B1-B12.",
